  <!-- PART I -->
  <div id="part1">
    <span class="part-tag">Part I · 101 First Principles</span>
    <h2>1. Knowledge as Lack of Ignorance: Observation Fibers</h2>
    <p>Consider any agent interacting with an environment whose full history belongs to a type $H$. The agent does not see $h \in H$ directly; it receives a partial observation $y = o(h) \in O$. The agent's exact epistemic state is the <strong>observation fiber</strong> — the preimage of $y$ under $o$:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\mathrm{Fib}_o(y) \;=\; \sum_{h : H} \big(o(h) \equiv y\big)$$</div>
    <p>Every new observation $o_2(h) = y_2$ intersects the fiber, eliminating histories incompatible with reality. Knowledge is literally a <em>lack of ignorance</em>: shrinking $\mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$ without ever fabricating a point inside it. In Hyperactivity, a claim $P : H \to \mathrm{Type}$ is <strong>warranted</strong> at observation $y$ if and only if it holds over the <em>entire</em> fiber:</p>
    <div class="eq-box">$$\mathrm{Warranted}(o, y, P) \;:\equiv\; \prod_{h : H} \big(o(h) \equiv y\big) \to P(h)$$</div>
    <p>Hallucination is now mathematically impossible to hide: asserting $P$ when $\mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$ contains two histories $(h_1, h_2)$ with $P(h_1)$ true and $\neg P(h_2)$ false is an explicit <strong>split witness</strong>. Instead of guessing, a rational agent issues an <strong>active query</strong> that separates $h_1$ from $h_2$.</p>

    <div class="lab-card">
      <div class="lab-head">
        <span class="lbl">◈ Interactive Lab 1 · Observation-Fiber &amp; Active-Querying Refinement Network</span>
        <span class="lab-status" id="fibStatus">8 compatible histories · SPLIT WITNESS (h₀ vs h₄)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-stage" id="fibStage"><canvas id="fibCanvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="lab-ctrl">
        <button class="lab-btn" id="qA">Query A: Parity</button>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="qB">Query B: Safety Bound</button>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="qC">Query C: Standpoint</button>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="qReset">Reset Fiber</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PART II -->
  <div id="part2">
    <span class="part-tag">Part II · Modern Research Frontier</span>
    <h2>2. Why Token-Stream LLMs, RLHF &amp; Lexical Safety Gates Fail by Construction</h2>
    <p>Modern frontier AI sits atop 1970s computing's original sin: untyped byte streams with broken equality. A Large Language Model learns a conditional distribution $p_\theta(x_t \mid x_{&lt;t})$ over raw byte tokens — forced to re-derive logic, arithmetic, and identity statistically on every forward pass because the substrate cannot carry checked types or proof objects.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Hallucination is structural:</strong> Sampling a single completion from $p_\theta$ commits to one point in an uncollapsed fiber $\mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$ without checking whether $P(h)$ is invariant across $\mathrm{Fib}_o(y)$.</li>
      <li><strong>Population-marginal RLHF destroys standpoint:</strong> Aggregating human preferences across incompatible values into a single scalar reward $r(x, y)$ averages contradictory standpoints into a muddled, sycophantic mean (violating Jain <em>Anekāntavāda</em> — many-sidedness — where every standpoint is carried explicitly as a typed perspective, never silently blended).</li>
      <li><strong>Lexical refusal gates fail framing invariance:</strong> A safety filter keyed to surface words $w \in \Sigma^*$ violates semantic equivalence: two terms $t_1 \equiv_{\mathrm{sem}} t_2$ with identical meaning receive opposite refusals under harmless rephrasing, while harmful intent bypasses lexical triggers.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="lab-card">
      <div class="lab-head">
        <span class="lbl">◈ Interactive Lab 2 · Standpoint Interaction Network: RLHF Averaging vs. Hyperactive Set-Union</span>
        <span class="lab-status" id="spStatus">Mode: Hyperactive Fiber Merge (zero standpoint erasure)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-stage" id="spStage"><canvas id="spCanvas"></canvas></div>
      <div class="lab-ctrl">
        <button class="lab-btn on" id="spModeHx">Hyperactive Set-Union</button>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="spModeRlhf">Scalar RLHF Average</button>
        <button class="lab-btn" id="spFlip">Flip Agent Standpoint</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PART III -->
  <div id="part3">
    <span class="part-tag">Part III · Computational Closure in Hyperactivity</span>
    <h2>3. Exact Warrant, Univalent Transport &amp; Zero-Search Compression</h2>
    <p>In Hyperactivity, multi-agent AI is modeled directly as a concurrent <strong>interaction network</strong> over content-addressed objects (<code>lib/observation.hypercubical</code>, <code>lib/obs.hypercubical</code>). Because every value is immutable and content-addressed, concurrent agents converge by pure set union with no canonical server state.</p>

    <div class="thm-box">
      <div class="thm-head"><span>Zero-Search Causal Context-Fiber Compression</span><span><code>lib/compress.hypercubical</code> · <code>lib/compress-stream.hypercubical</code></span></div>
      <p>Intelligence is compression: discovering exact structural identities that collapse redundancy. In <code>lib/compress.hypercubical</code> and <code>lib/compress-stream.hypercubical</code>, the exact same observation-fiber machinery produces a zero-search, <code>refl</code>-checked lossless compressor over streaming $O(N)$ renormalized rANS with zero probability axioms and zero serialized lookup tables — outperforming <code>gzip -9</code>, <code>bzip2 -9</code>, and <code>lzma</code> on real byte streams (see <a href="/compression">/compression</a>).</p>
    </div>

    <p>Once a theorem, safety certificate, or algorithmic optimization is checked once in the store, <strong>univalence (<code>ua</code>)</strong> and verified specialization (<code>lib/specialize.hypercubical</code>) transport it across equivalent representations automatically, while <code>lib/credit.hypercubical</code> tracks conserved multi-dimensional credit across cooperating agents without a blockchain or 1D scalar money.</p>
  </div>

